copygc, data/update: fix durability_keeping mismatch livelock and EC copygc

On pools combining replication and erasure coding (or degraded EC stripes),
copygc can enter a 100%-deterministic non-progressing livelock that pins
bch-copygc on CPU, keeps bch2_copygc_can_make_progress() true on full
devices, and permanently stalls foreground allocations and reconcile.

Four interacting bugs cause or compound this stall:

1. Whole-key vs per-pointer durability mismatch in data_update:
   bch2_data_update_init() and bch2_can_do_data_update() compute
   durability_keeping by summing bch2_extent_ptr_durability() independently
   per kept pointer (returning .total with no cross-pointer device
   deduplication). When kept pointers share devices across EC stripes and/or
   plain replicas, or when a stripe block is offline so .online < .total,
   durability_keeping >= io_opts->data_replicas sets m->op.nr_replicas = 0
   and takes the no-write bch2_extent_drop_ptrs() path.
   bch2_extent_drop_ptrs() delegates to bch2_bkey_drop_extra_durability() /
   bch2_bkey_drop_extra_ec_durability(), which evaluate whole-key
   bch2_bkey_durability().online WITH @seen device deduplication, find
   durability.online < data_replicas, refuse to drop the pointer, commit an
   unchanged key, and return 0 -> data_update_done_no_writes_needed.
   Copygc treats that no-op as completed work (sectors_seen > 0 -> did_work
   == true), skips io_clock backoff, and spins forever on the same LRU head
   buckets (~3,000 no-op evacuations/sec observed in production).

   Fix: introduce bch2_bkey_durability_keeping(), which computes whole-key
   deduplicated online durability over the kept pointers (matching
   bch2_bkey_drop_extra_*_durability()) and adds @seen.online devices to
   devs_have so newly allocated replacement replicas are guaranteed to land
   on non-overlapping devices that increase whole-key durability. Also guard
   bch2_extent_drop_ptrs() against ever committing an unchanged key and
   reporting data_update_done_no_writes_needed.

2. No-op / failed evacuations counted as sectors_seen in move.c:
   __bch2_move_data_phys() unconditionally added bucket_len to sectors_seen
   even when pred() returned 0 (e.g. bucket generation mismatch) or when
   bch2_move_extent() returned BCH_ERR_data_update_fail, and double-counted
   sectors when stats->phys == false (copygc). Only count sectors_seen in
   __bch2_move_data_phys() when stats->phys is true, and in
   bch2_move_extent() when !ret, so copygc's did_work backoff triggers when
   no extents were actually processed.

3. Broken EC stripe copygc selection and positive return leak in copygc.c:
   - try_add_copygc_bucket() returns 1 when a bucket is added to the batch;
     bch2_copygc_get_stripe_buckets() failed to clear ret2 = 1 when the
     stripe had fewer blocks than the batch size (16), returning 1 from
     bch2_copygc_get_stripe_buckets() and causing bch2_copygc()'s `if (ret)
     goto err;` check to abort and free the batch without evacuating anything.
   - stripe_lru_pos() scores empty slots + s->can_widen, placing 100%-full
     widenable stripes (blocks_nonempty == nr_data) at the head of
     BCH_LRU_STRIPE_FRAGMENTATION; should_do_ec_copygc() inspected only the
     first non-empty entry (returning false forever on a full can_widen
     stripe) and evaluated `stripe_frag_ratio * 2 < bucket_frag_ratio` as
     false when bucket_frag_ratio == 0 (devices with fragmented stripes and
     zero fragmented normal buckets).
   Fix all three, filter stripes to those holding blocks on pressured
   devices, and bound skipped LRU entries at 512.

4. bch2_copygc_can_make_progress() ignored c->opts.copygc_enabled, leaving
   foreground allocators waiting forever if copygc_enabled is disabled via
   sysfs.

